<p>As shown in Figure 11, the producer tries to send messages to partition =
1 of topic A.</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>The producer sends messages to the routing layer.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The routing layer reads the replica distribution plan<sup><a id=3D"user-=
content-fnref-1" data-footnote-ref=3D"true" aria-describedby=3D"footnote-la=
bel" href=3D"https://bytebytego.com/courses/system-design-interview/distrib=
uted-message-queue#user-content-fn-1">1</a></sup> from the metadata storage=
 and caches it locally. When a message arrives, it routes the message to th=
e leader replica of partition 1, which is stored in broker 1.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>The leader replica receives the message and follower replicas pull data =
from the leader.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>When =E2=80=9Cenough=E2=80=9D replicas have synchronized the message, th=
e leader commits the data (persisted on disk), which means the data can be =
consumed. Then it responds to the producer.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>You might be wondering why we need both leader and follower replicas. Th=
e reason is fault tolerance. We dive deep into this process in the =E2=80=
=9CIn-sync replicas=E2=80=9D section.</p>
<p>This approach works, but it has a few drawbacks:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>A new routing layer means additional network latency caused by overhead =
and additional network hops.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Request batching is one of the big drivers of efficiency. This design do=
esn=E2=80=99t take that into consideration.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Figure 12 shows the improved design.</p>
