XQ6W.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; =
justify-content: center;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 24 How ISR work=
s</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>Why do we need ISR? The reason is that ISR reflects the trade-off betwee=
n performance and durability. If producers don=E2=80=99t want to lose any m=
essages, the safest way to do that is to ensure all replicas are already in=
 sync before sending an acknowledgment. But a slow replica will cause the w=
hole partition to become slow or unavailable.</p>
<p>Now that we=E2=80=99ve discussed ISR, let=E2=80=99s take a look at ackno=
wledgment settings. Producers can choose to receive acknowledgments until t=
he <em>K</em> number of ISRs has received the message, where <em>K</em> is =
configurable.</p>
<p><strong>ACK=3Dall</strong></p>
<p>Figure 25 illustrates the case with ACK=3Dall. With ACK=3Dall, the produ=
cer gets an ACK when all ISRs have received the message. This means it take=
s a longer time to send a message because we need to wait for the slowest I=
SR, but it gives the strongest message durability.</p>
